[IMP] base: EXPLAIN ANALYSE queries from profile

The profiling feature is really cool to analyse performance issues and detect
bottlenecks.
However, once you've identified a slow query, there's nothing to help you.

You need to:
* copy-paste the speedscope entry with the SQL query
* extract the query from the line
* have ssh access to the server
* manually run EXPLAIN ANALYSE

This commit introduces a way to run EXPLAIN ANALYSE on any profiled query.

Design decisions
*---------------
We are keeping the current schema, with SQL stored in a serialized blob.
This allows external tools (runbot, ...) to work the same way across versions.
It also allows to add metrics easily in the blob in stable versions if
necessary.
Preserving the schema also makes it possible to backport this feature to
stable versions in the future, as we could simply extract the feature and move it to a
separate module.

Upon request, we duplicate the SQL data in a separate model (`ir.profile.query`)
so it can be displayed and manipulated with standard list/form views.
Duplicating the data is acceptable since it's throwaway data that is regularly
garbage-collected.

Visualizer?
*----------
We considered including dalibo's open-source visualizer (pev2) to be able
to visualize plans in one click.
However, because that component depends on Vue.js, including it would likely
trigger automated vulnerability scanners. Even if it's secure, we want to
avoid the overhead of processing false-positive security reports.
As a general rule, we also avoid adding external dependencies whenever possible.

Instead, users can take the generated execution plan and visualize it as
they see fit (e.g., using explain.dalibo.com). (Please note that SQL queries
may contain sensitive data!)

Accepted risks
*-------------
Some side-effects can escape the savepoint.

The savepoint rollback only undoes what is transactional. Anything
non-transactional survives: sequence advances (setval, nextval),
session-level advisory locks (pg_advisory_lock and friends), etc.

This is accepted because it grants no privilege that the caller does not
already have: the feature is restricted to system users, who can already
run arbitrary SQL through crons, server actions.
A block-list would be hard to maintain.

This feature is obviously limited to admin users.

